cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata below. Please also
  cite the datasets and methods it builds on, listed under `references`.
title: >-
  sperm-sorting-ai: real-time AI-guided sperm analysis and conditional magnetic
  sorting (research prototype)
abstract: >-
  A research prototype that analyses visible phenotype - presence, trajectory,
  velocity, direction, progression, linearity and morphology - from monochrome
  microscopy video, and emits a two-state FIELD_ON / FIELD_OFF command for a
  conditional magnetic sorter. It does not measure DNA integrity,
  phosphatidylserine exposure, Annexin V binding, apoptosis, magnetic labelling,
  fertility potential or pregnancy rate. Research use only; not a medical
  device; not clinically validated; not validated against WHO 6th edition manual
  assessment. See docs/safety_and_claims.md.
type: software
version: 0.1.0
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: https://github.com/hannan72/sperm_sorting
keywords:
  - microscopy
  - computer-vision
  - multi-object-tracking
  - CASA
  - sperm-morphology
  - real-time
  - research-prototype
authors:
  - name: sperm_sorting contributors
    website: https://github.com/hannan72/sperm_sorting

references:
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------- standards
  - type: manual
    title: >-
      WHO laboratory manual for the examination and processing of human semen
    edition: Sixth edition
    year: 2021
    publisher:
      name: World Health Organization
    authors:
      - name: World Health Organization
    isbn: '9789240030787'
    url: https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240030787
    notes: >-
      Source of the four-category motility grading and its approximate velocity
      limits (section 2.4.6.1, 25 um/s and 5 um/s), the requirement that the
      sample be held at 37 C (section 2.4.6), the morphology criteria and
      morphometry (Table 2.6), the CASA parameter definitions (section 4.5.1.4)
      and the statement that cross-instrument comparability "is not yet known"
      (Figure 4.4), the reference limits (Table 8.3) and the statement that they
      are not a fertile/infertile boundary (section 8.1.3), and the Annexin V
      MACS protocol (section 5.6.2). Verified against the official PDF; see
      docs/source_audit.md section 4.

  # ----------------------------------------------------------------- datasets
  - type: article
    title: >-
      VISEM-Tracking, a human spermatozoa tracking dataset
    journal: Scientific Data
    year: 2023
    authors:
      - name: >-
          VISEM-Tracking dataset authors (SimulaMet HOST)
    url: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7293726
    notes: >-
      Also available as arXiv:2212.02842; data on Zenodo record 7293726, licensed
      CC BY 4.0. 20 annotated 30-second clips from 20 patients; 29,196 annotated
      frames; 656,334 bounding boxes; 1,121 unique sperm track IDs. Official
      split is 16 train / 4 validation by video, with validation video IDs 82,
      60, 54 and 52; there is no official test split. AUTHOR LIST INCOMPLETE -
      the individual authors were not recorded during the source audit and must
      be taken from the published paper before this entry is used for citation.
      See docs/dataset_audit.md section 2.

  - type: data
    title: 'MHSMA: Modified Human Sperm Morphology Analysis dataset'
    year: 2018
    authors:
      - name: >-
          MHSMA dataset authors (github.com/soroushj/mhsma-dataset)
    url: https://github.com/soroushj/mhsma-dataset
    license: CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0
    notes: >-
      1540 grayscale single-sperm crops (1000 train / 240 valid / 300 test) from
      235 male-factor-infertility patients, at 128x128 and 64x64, with four
      independent binary morphology labels (head, acrosome, vacuole, tail) using
      0 = normal and 1 = abnormal. NOTE the dataset README calls the *normal*
      class "positive". Derived from HSMA-DS (Ghasemian 2015,
      doi:10.1016/j.cmpb.2015.08.013). It is NOT on Zenodo; Zenodo record 2640506
      is VISEM. AUTHOR LIST INCOMPLETE - only the repository owner's handle was
      recorded during the source audit; complete from the primary source before
      citing. See docs/dataset_audit.md section 1.

  # ------------------------------------------------------------------ methods
  - type: article
    title: >-
      TOD-CNN: an effective convolutional neural network for tiny object
      detection in sperm videos
    year: 2022
    authors:
      - family-names: Chen
    url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.08166
    notes: >-
      Preprint arXiv:2204.08166. Source of the design argument reimplemented
      independently in src/sperm_sorting/detection/todcnn.py - no weights, no
      exported graph and no layer-by-layer transcription were taken. Associated
      dataset MIaMIA-SVDS (figshare record 15074253); code repository
      github.com/Demozsj/Detection-Sperm. AUTHOR LIST INCOMPLETE - only the first
      author's family name is recorded in the code and in the source audit.

  - type: article
    title: >-
      The future of computer-aided sperm analysis
    journal: Asian Journal of Andrology
    year: 2015
    volume: '17'
    issue: '4'
    start: 545
    end: 553
    authors:
      - family-names: Mortimer
        given-names: S. T.
      - family-names: van der Horst
        given-names: G.
      - family-names: Mortimer
        given-names: D.
    url: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4492043/
    notes: >-
      Open access; PMC4492043. Source of the ~60 images/s minimum imaging
      frequency figure, which is THIS PAPER'S and not WHO's - the WHO 6th edition
      specifies no numeric minimum frame rate. Also the source of the finding
      that a fixed five-point average-path smoother "will provide inadequate
      smoothing and hence widely aberrant ALH values" once the frame rate
      changes, which is why MotionConfig.vap_window_ms is specified as a duration
      rather than a frame count.

  - type: article
    title: >-
      The 2021 WHO laboratory manual for the examination and processing of human
      semen: is it a step toward better standard operating procedure?
    journal: Fertility and Sterility
    year: 2022
    volume: '117'
    issue: '2'
    start: 246
    end: 251
    authors:
      - family-names: Björndahl
      - family-names: Kirkman-Brown
    notes: >-
      Open access, CC BY. Quoted verbatim in docs/safety_and_claims.md section 8
      on emerging semen-analysis technologies asserting equivalence to WHO core
      methods, and the source of the observation that a rapidly progressive
      spermatozoon can equivalently be described as one moving more than five
      head-lengths per second - which at a 4.1 um head is 20.5 um/s, against half
      a tail length at 22.5 um and the manual's own 25 um/s. GIVEN NAMES NOT
      RECORDED during the source audit; complete from the primary source before
      citing.
